### How it satisfies every requirement

| Requirement | How it's met |
|---|---|
| **Public `recordId`, reactive on change** | `@api recordId` makes the property reactive. The wire config uses the `$recordId` token, so every time the parent (record page / console tab) assigns a new value, `getRecord` automatically re-fires with the new id. |
| **`getRecord` wire, four fields** | The `@wire(getRecord, …)` decorator calls `getRecord` with a **module-level** `const FIELDS` array containing the four field paths. A module-level constant is critical: an inline array would be a new reference every render, causing an infinite re-wire loop. |
| **Spinner while loading (no data, no error)** | The `isLoading` getter returns `true` only when **both** `data` and `error` are falsy. The `<template if:true={isLoading}>` block renders the spinner and nothing else during that window. |
| **Data block: `<h1>`, optional `<p>` industry, `<lightning-formatted-number>`, `<p>` owner** | Wrapped in `<template if:true={account}>`. Industry is guarded by an inner `if:true={account.Industry}` so the `<p>` is omitted when null/blank. Revenue uses `format-style="currency"` and binds `value` to the numeric field. Owner renders unconditionally inside the data block per the spec. |
| **Error block: `<p class="error">` with `error.body.message`, no spinner** | Separate `<template if:true={accountError}>` renders the error paragraph. Because `isLoading` is `false` when `error` is truthy, the spinner block is skipped. |
| **No Apex** | All data comes through `lightning/uiRecordApi`'s `getRecord` wire adapter. |

